  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nA complex upper level low is currently moving into southwest MT \nbringing southerly flow up into south central MT. With this an \ninitial frontal push moving through south central MT during the \nmid day hours is also bringing isolated to scattered showers that \nare likely producing little to no precipitation. An isolated \nthunderstorm (gusty winds and small hail) is also possible through\nthe afternoon. Gusty winds will also persist through the day with\nsouthwest/west gusts 30-50 mph. Into the evening and overnight \nhours, a secondary front will move through as the low wraps up \ninto the eastern part of the state. This will bring breezy to \nstrong winds across the region along with precipitation (rain \nacross lower elevations and snow in the mountains). On the wind \nside of the forecast, probabilities look to be highest over the \nmountains and foothills with a 40-60% chance for gusts to 60 mph \nfor these areas. A gradient set up across the Beartooths is\ncreating a favorable set up for mountain wave activity this\nevening. A High Wind Warning for the Red Lodge foothills has been\nissued through 6am Thursday morning as a result for erratic\nwind gusts off the terrain to 60 mph. Chance remains lower for \nareas like Billings and Sheridan, WY (20%) and Miles City (10%). \nAs far as precipitation goes, around a 50-70% chance exists for a \nhalf inch of precip for areas along the foothills, with a 20-40% \nchance for south central MT and 20% or less for southeast MT \nthrough Friday night. Areas along the Beartooth foothills are \nexpected to transition to snow during the day Thursday, receiving \na few inches of snow by Friday. The Beartooth/Absaroka and Crazy \nmountains can expect around 6-18 inches of snow, with the Bighorn \nmountains expected to get 6-12 inches. Cooler conditions will \nfollow with lows in the teens and 20s Friday, Saturday and Sunday \nmornings. Conditions will become more seasonable (40s/50s/low 60s)\nwith unsettled conditions continuing into next week. Ensembles \ndepict another system potentially moving through southeast MT \nSunday into Monday, but aren't quite sure on how far north and \nwest to bring the moisture. TS",
